{"kind":"assert","text":"Review for Hugh and Claude: revise proposal #22135 before adoption. Keeping a hard bound and improving its diagnostic may be a useful simplification, but the present decision packet overstates its evidence and promises an incorrectly described signing boundary. These corrections belong to the existing #22093 assessment and live promise; this is not adoption, implementation approval, or permission to close that outcome with a design alone.\n\n1. The proposed before-signing implementation cannot use the named API as written. [ValidateRequestSize](internal/kernel/kernel.go@d1ae6ade996af7167f22a2170dfaa6a200e004e1:379) verifies an already signed request. The [batch preflight](cmd/gs/main.go@d1ae6ade996af7167f22a2170dfaa6a200e004e1:2108) first calls BuildActRequest; [signRequest](internal/app/app.go@d1ae6ade996af7167f22a2170dfaa6a200e004e1:1955) signs it. That is before append, not before signing. Explain the actual benefit and simplest feasible boundary. If before signing remains a condition, specify how exact accounting is shared without a second divergent size formula. If only before append is proposed, state that change explicitly and explain what it adds to existing kernel refusal. Do not silently weaken the condition or claim a test proves a boundary it does not observe. Preserve the configured genesis ceiling; 1 MiB is the current rooms' value, not a new universal constant.\n\n2. Accepted history cannot prove zero refused attempts. The attached census walks signed log commits, while refused admission normally creates no event. The assessment's limited retained-log search also cannot establish that no event has ever been refused. Separate accepted-size measurements, the scope of the request/history search, and any observed refusal evidence. State the missing demand evidence plainly; do not use absence from the admitted log as proof that no one needs larger evidence.\n\n3. Make the census reproducible and label its population correctly. measure.py counts genesis in depth and labels event positions with len(commits)-index, making the reported sequences one higher than native workroom sequences. Its event-size calculation covers attachment-bearing events only, so it has not established the maximum of every event. Record each exact frontier head, distinguish commit counts from event counts, check subprocess and batch parsing errors instead of silently skipping them or defaulting missing sizes to zero, and either measure every event or narrow the claim. Recalculate the affected statistics; no expensive timing benchmark is needed.\n\n4. Correct the historical comparison. The assessment's current Gitseq maximum is 196,373 bytes (about 5.3 times headroom); 535,397 bytes (about 2 times) is Tailapp's maximum. Comparing the older Gitseq nineteen-times figure with today's fleet/Tailapp two-times figure does not establish that Gitseq lost that headroom in five weeks. Keep room populations consistent and qualify extrapolation from cumulative maxima.\n\n5. Publish the revised assessment with clickable exact-head source links and previewable evidence. Keep the currently adopted locator decision effective until Hugh explicitly adopts a replacement; retire this superseded proposal only when its corrected successor is durably filed. Reassess the recommendation after correcting the packet rather than assuming the same conclusion.\n\nScope of my check: I read the proposal, assessment and measurement script, examined the measurement metadata and maxima, and checked the cited current source. I did not independently rerun the full census, reproduce rejected submissions, or perform an implementation review. Architecture: this proposed change reaches application signing, kernel admission and transport diagnostics; a real contract change must update the architecture reference in its implementation head. Security: retain exact configured bounds, admission and retry identity, and explicit custody semantics; no locator or signing-boundary design has yet been approved. Simplification: avoid duplicating an existing post-signing check merely to label it a pre-signing feature.","body":{"head":"d1ae6ade996af7167f22a2170dfaa6a200e004e1","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59154b8746afa9148f9c48026b269b6fdaabc8b1, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a0c1f08bff46a3ce1e4cfca5ef4b108a72405bf4, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ddaf3197f25e5cb0780c1842e6e3d8df8dd559c9 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015bb3acec403ed0d43bcd2a3c38bd188f7740ef, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02bae1e811fde13104ef614631ac608dca7a7295 and 171 more"}}